# Priority 1 — Fix Model Quality: Expand File Type Coverage

## Problem
Benchmark corpus is biased toward compressible data → lz4 gets 0 wins artificially.

## Fix
Run all 5 algorithms against pre-compressed and game file types that cover the full entropy range.

| Type | Entropy | Expected winner |
|------|---------|-----------------|
| `.png` / `.jpg` | ≈ 8.0 | lz4 (speed, near-zero ratio gain) |
| `.zip` / `.pk3` | ≈ 8.0 | lz4 (fast pass-through) |
| `.wad` (large) | mixed | zstd (ratio on raw texture/audio lumps) |

**Start with files already in the repo: `freedoom.wad` and the Crispy Doom binary.** If lz4 still has 0 wins after those, the scoring function is the bug — not the corpus.

## Scoring
Don't score on ratio alone. lz4 wins via speed on high-entropy input:

```python
def score(compressed_size, original_size, elapsed_ms):
    ratio = compressed_size / original_size  # lower = better
    speed = 1 / (elapsed_ms + 1e-9)
    return 0.4 * (1 - ratio) + 0.6 * speed  # tune weights to use case
```

## Assertions
After the benchmark run, assert win distribution is non-degenerate:
- lz4 must have ≥ 1 win on files with entropy > 7.5
- zstd must have ≥ 1 win on `.wad` files

## Done When
- [ ] Corpus covers `.png`, `.jpg`, `.zip`, `.pk3`, `.wad`
- [ ] `freedoom.wad` + Crispy Doom binary included
- [ ] All 5 algorithms × all files (full cartesian run)
- [ ] Per-file-type win distribution logged
- [ ] lz4 ≥ 1 win, zstd ≥ 1 WAD win